    IDS_ADDKEYWORDS         "Add Keywords or categories"
    IDS_ADDKEYWORDS_DESC    "Add Keywords or categories to images\nAdd Keywords or categories to images"
    IDS_PUBLISHIMAGES       "Publish Images"
    IDS_JPEG_TITLE          "Joint Photographic Experts Group JFIF"
    IDS_JPEG_DESC           "JPEG (pronounced 'jay-peg') is a standardized image compression mechanism.  JPEG stands for Joint Photographic Experts Group, the original name of the committee that wrote the standard.\r\n\r\nJPEG is designed for compressing either full-color or gray-scale images of natural, real-world scenes. It works well on photographs, naturalistic artwork, and similar material; not so well on lettering, simple cartoons, or line drawings. JPEG handles only still images, but there is a related standard called MPEG for motion pictures.\r\n\r\nJPEG is 'lossy,' meaning that the decompressed image isn't quite the same as the one you started with. (There are lossless image compression algorithms, but JPEG achieves much greater compression than is possible with lossless methods.) "
    IDS_J2K_TITLE           "JPEG-2000 File Format Syntax"
END

STRINGTABLE 
BEGIN
    IDS_J2K_DESC            "JPEG 2000 is a new image coding system that uses state-of-the-art compression techniques based on wavelet technology."
    IDS_BMP_TITLE           "Microsoft Windows bitmap"
    IDS_BMP_DESC            "A BMP file is commonly referred to as a bitmap. This format is typically not compressed and is compatible with many programs that run on Microsoft Windows."
    IDS_GIF_TITLE           "CompuServe graphics interchange"
    IDS_GIF_DESC            "GIF format used often for displaying bitmap images on World Wide Web pages. The format only supports 256 colors but can support simple animation."
    IDS_PNG_TITLE           "Portable Network Graphics"
    IDS_PNG_DESC            "The Portable Network Graphics (PNG) format was designed to replace the older and simpler GIF format and, to some extent, the much more complex TIFF format.\r\n\r\nFor the Web, PNG really has three main advantages over GIF: alpha channels (variable transparency), gamma correction (cross-platform control of image brightness), and two-dimensional interlacing (a method of progressive display). PNG also compresses better than GIF in almost every case, but the difference is generally only around 5% to 25%, not a large enough factor to encourage folks to switch on that basis alone. One GIF feature that PNG does not try to reproduce is multiple-image support, especially animations; PNG was and is intended to be a single-image format only. "
    IDS_TIFF_TITLE          "Tagged Image File"
    IDS_TIFF_DESC           "TIFF's are one of the most widely supported file formats for storing bit-mapped images on personal computers (both PCs and Macintosh computers).\r\n\r\nTIFF graphics can be any resolution, and they can be black and white, gray-scaled, or color.\r\n\r\nA primary goal of TIFF is to provide a rich environment within which applications can exchange image data. This richness is required to take advantage of the varying capabilities of scanners and other imaging devices. Though TIFF is a rich format, it can easily be used for simple scanners and applications as well because the number of required fields is small."
    IDS_WMF_TITLE           "Windows Meta File"
    IDS_WMF_DESC            "This is a Vector Graphic often used for storing clip art images in Word Processing documents."
    IDS_CDF_TITLE           "Compound Doc File"
    IDS_CDF_DESC            "Compound Doc File such as Word or Excel"
    IDS_MISC_TITLE          "Various Images"
    IDS_MISC_DESC           "Various Images"
    IDS_HISTOGRAM_EQUALIZATION_DESC 
                            "Histogram Equalization\nHistogram Equalization"
END

